/*
    APPROACH:

    We are allowed to perform at most 3 operations.

    In each operation, we can change any element to any value.

    Our goal is to minimise the difference between the
    maximum and minimum elements of the array.

    IDEA:

    1. First, sort the array in ascending order.

    2. After sorting, the smallest elements are on the left
       and the largest elements are on the right.

    3. Since we have 3 operations, we can change at most
       3 extreme elements to bring the difference down.

    4. We have 4 possible ways to distribute these 3 operations:

       Case 1: Change the 3 smallest elements.
       Case 2: Change 1 smallest and 2 largest elements.
       Case 3: Change 2 smallest and 1 largest element.
       Case 4: Change the 3 largest elements.

    5. Calculate the remaining difference for all 4 cases.

    6. Return the minimum among these 4 differences.

    TIME COMPLEXITY: O(n log n) because of sorting.
    SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(log n) auxiliary space for sorting.
*/

class Solution {
    public int minDifference(int[] arr) {

        // Store the size of the array
        int n = arr.length;

        // If the array has at most 3 elements,
        // we can make all elements equal in 3 operations
        if (n == 1 || n == 2 || n == 3) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Sort the array in ascending order
        Arrays.sort(arr);

        // Case 1: Change the 3 smallest elements
        // Remaining elements are from index 3 to n-1
        int op1 = arr[n-1] - arr[3];

        // Case 2: Change 1 smallest and 2 largest elements
        // Remaining elements are from index 1 to n-3
        int op2 = arr[n-3] - arr[1];

        // Case 3: Change 2 smallest and 1 largest element
        // Remaining elements are from index 2 to n-2
        int op3 = arr[n-2] - arr[2];

        // Case 4: Change the 3 largest elements
        // Remaining elements are from index 0 to n-4
        int op4 = arr[n-4] - arr[0];

        // Return the minimum difference among all 4 cases
        return Math.min(Math.min(op1, op2), Math.min(op3, op4));
    }
}
